HARD = {

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Introduction and business models
"b-intro": [
 ("In a used-car market 40% of cars are peaches and 60% are lemons. Buyers value a peach at R90 000 and a lemon at R40 000 but cannot tell them apart; peach owners will not sell below R80 000 and lemon owners will not sell below R30 000. Buyers are rational and know what sellers will do. At what price do cars actually trade once the market settles (rand)?",
  "R40 000. Step 1: a random car is worth 0.4 x 90 000 + 0.6 x 40 000 = R60 000 to a buyer. Step 2: peach owners need R80 000, so at R60 000 they withdraw. Step 3: buyers then know only lemons are on offer, so they pay at most R40 000. Trap: R60 000 is only the opening bid; adverse selection drives the good cars out and the price falls to the lemon value.", 40000),
 ("In a used-car market buyers value a peach at R90 000 and a lemon at R40 000 but cannot tell them apart, and peach owners will not sell below R80 000. What is the minimum percentage of peaches in the market for peach owners to keep selling, so that the market does not unravel (percent, whole number)?",
  "80%. Buyers pay the expected value p x 90 000 + (1 - p) x 40 000, which must be at least R80 000: 40 000 + 50 000p >= 80 000, so p >= 0.80. With fewer than 80% peaches the pooled price is below the peach owners' reserve price, they leave, and only lemons trade. This is why lenders need information or collateral to separate good borrowers from bad.", 80),
 ("A bank lends at 14% to a pool in which 3% of borrowers default. It raises the rate to 22% to 'price for risk', but safe borrowers leave and the pool that remains has a 15% default rate. On default the bank recovers 40% of the amount lent (and no interest); otherwise it is repaid in full with interest. What is the bank's expected return per rand lent at the 22% rate (percent, 2 decimals)?",
  "9.70%. At 22%: 0.85 x 1.22 + 0.15 x 0.40 = 1.037 + 0.060 = 1.097, so 9.70%. At 14% it was 0.97 x 1.14 + 0.03 x 0.40 = 1.1178, i.e. 11.78%. The higher rate LOWERS the expected return: it drives out good borrowers (adverse selection) and raises the debt burden of those who stay. This is why the loan supply curve bends backwards and banks ration credit instead of simply charging more.", 9.7),
 ("A bank cannot tell safe from risky small-business applicants, so it quotes every applicant the same 18%. Safe firms can borrow from their suppliers at about 15%. (a) Which applicants will take the bank's loan, (b) what is this problem called, and (c) name the two remedies the course gives.",
  "(a) Mostly the risky firms: 18% is too dear for safe firms, who go elsewhere, so the pool the bank ends up with is worse than average and 18% no longer covers its risk. (b) Adverse selection, a hidden-attributes problem that arises BEFORE the loan is made (the lemons problem). (c) Disclosure or production of information (credit bureaus, audited statements, the bank's own transaction data), and collateral or net worth, which gives the lender recourse and makes a good borrower's promise credible.",
  [r"adverse selection", r"collateral|net worth|information|disclos|credit bureau"]),
 ("150 teachers who belong to the same union have saved R1.2m together and want a member-owned institution that takes their savings and makes low-cost loans to members only, with no outside shareholders. Which type of bank fits, and can they register it today? Give the reason.",
  "A cooperative bank fits: members share a common bond (the union), it serves members only and it is not run for profit. They cannot register yet: the legal minimum is 200 members and they have 150 (the R1m minimum deposits test is met). A mutual bank needs only 7 founders and no common bond, but it needs R10m of capital and also serves outside clients, so it does not fit. Trap: the common bond belongs to cooperative banks, not mutual banks.",
  [r"co-?op", r"200"]),
 ("A Kenyan bank has an approved representative office in Sandton. A mining client asks that office to open a rand deposit account and grant a R50m loan. Can the office do this? If not, what is the minimum form of presence the Kenyan bank needs, and what is a representative office actually for?",
  "No. A representative office cannot do any banking business: no deposits, no loans. It exists to market the brand, gather information on client needs and build a relationship with the SARB before applying to operate. To take deposits and lend, the bank needs at least a local branch of a foreign bank (a fully functional office), or a locally registered bank.",
  [NO, r"branch"]),
 ("An app collects R200 from each of 5 000 savers, who may withdraw on demand, scores a property developer using his bank-statement data, and lends him the R1m for three years. (a) Name the three intermediary functions being performed. (b) Is this direct or indirect finance? (c) Which feature exposes the app to a run?",
  "(a) Pooling (many small savers fund one large loan), collecting and processing information (the credit score) and maturity intermediation (on-demand funding of a three-year loan). (b) Indirect finance: the savers hold a claim on the app, not on the developer. (c) The maturity mismatch: savers can all ask for their money long before the loan is repaid, so the app faces liquidity risk. An intermediary reduces risk and information asymmetry; it does not eliminate them.",
  [r"pool", r"inform", r"maturit|liquid|mismatch"]),
 ("A bank's investment banking team is advising a listed retailer on a confidential takeover bid. The same bank's private banking division is about to recommend the target company's shares to wealthy clients, and its corporate bankers lend to the bidder. What control must the bank have in place, what is the risk if it fails, and why does this arise in investment banking in particular?",
  "Chinese walls: barriers that stop non-public information held in one department from influencing investment decisions in another. Without them the private bank could advise or trade on inside information (a conflict of interest, and market abuse). It arises in investment banking because advisory work on M&A, IPOs and capital raising gives the bank price-sensitive information about clients, while other divisions invest or lend in the same companies.",
  [r"chinese wall", r"non-?public|inside|confidential|conflict|price.sensitive"]),
],

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Bank financial statements and ratios
"b-fin": [
 ("Bank K (R million): loans and advances 620 000; government bonds 95 000; interbank placements 40 000; cash and balances with the central bank 45 000 (the central bank pays no interest); investments in associates 6 000; property and equipment 12 000. Interest income is 71 500 and interest expense 38 300. What is the NIM (percent, 2 decimals)?",
  "4.40%. NII = 71 500 - 38 300 = 33 200. Interest-earning assets = 620 000 + 95 000 + 40 000 = 755 000 (exclude the cash, which earns nothing, the associates and the property). NIM = 33 200 / 755 000 = 4.40%. Traps: dividing by total assets of 818 000 gives 4.06%; including the cash gives 4.15%; associates are never earning assets because their income is not part of NII.", 4.4),
 ("Bank K (R million) has NII of 33 200, non-interest revenue of 18 800, credit impairments of 6 200, operating expenses of 28 600 and a tax rate of 27%. Total assets are 818 000 and equity is 68 000. Calculate ROE (percent, 2 decimals).",
  "18.46%. Total income = 52 000. Profit before tax = 52 000 - 6 200 - 28 600 = 17 200; after tax = 17 200 x 0.73 = 12 556. ROE = 12 556 / 68 000 = 18.46%. DuPont check: profit margin 12 556 / 52 000 = 24.15%; asset utilisation 52 000 / 818 000 = 6.36%; leverage 818 000 / 68 000 = 12.03; product = 18.46%. ROA is only 1.53%, so the ROE depends on leverage of 12 times.", 18.46),
 ("Last year a bank had total income of R48 000m and operating expenses of R25 000m. This year total income is R52 000m and operating expenses are R28 600m. Calculate the jaws ratio (percentage points, 2 decimals; negative if costs grew faster than income).",
  "-6.07 points. Income growth = 52 000 / 48 000 - 1 = 8.33%. Cost growth = 28 600 / 25 000 - 1 = 14.40%. Jaws = 8.33% - 14.40% = -6.07. Negative jaws means the cost-to-income ratio worsened: from 25 000 / 48 000 = 52.1% to 28 600 / 52 000 = 55.0%. Trap: jaws is the difference between two growth rates, not the change in the cost-to-income ratio (2.9 points).", -6.07),
 ("A bank has total assets of R900 000m, equity of R75 000m and an ROA of 1.20%. It buys back R15 000m of its own shares, funded by issuing debt, so total assets do not change. The extra interest reduces profit after tax by R900m. What is the new ROE (percent, 2 decimals)?",
  "16.50%. Old profit = 1.20% x 900 000 = 10 800, so old ROE = 10 800 / 75 000 = 14.40%. New profit = 10 800 - 900 = 9 900; new equity = 75 000 - 15 000 = 60 000; new ROE = 9 900 / 60 000 = 16.50%. Trap: ROE rose although the bank became LESS profitable (ROA fell from 1.20% to 1.10%). The whole gain is leverage (12 times to 15 times), the Dis-Chem effect, and the bank is now more fragile.", 16.5),
 ("A bank has loans of R620 000m and customer deposits of R590 000m. Management wants the loans-to-deposits ratio down to 90% without shrinking the loan book. How much additional deposit funding must it raise (R million, nearest whole number)?",
  "R98 889m. Current LDR = 620 000 / 590 000 = 105.1%, above the prudent 80% to 90% range, so part of the loan book is funded by dearer wholesale debt. Deposits needed at 90% = 620 000 / 0.90 = 688 889. Extra deposits = 688 889 - 590 000 = 98 889. Trap: taking 90% of 620 000 (558 000) inverts the ratio; loans are the numerator.", 98889),
 ("A bank reports that NII grew 6% to a record level while interest-earning assets grew 11%. The CEO tells investors 'our margin has never been better'. Is he right? Explain what happened to NIM and give one plausible driver.",
  "He is wrong. NIM = NII / interest-earning assets, and the denominator grew faster than the numerator, so NIM fell (by about 1.06 / 1.11 - 1 = -4.5%, e.g. from 4.00% to 3.82%). Record NII only shows that the balance sheet is bigger. Plausible drivers: growth was in lower-yielding assets (government bonds, interbank placements, cash), loan pricing was cut to win volume, or funding costs rose faster than asset yields.",
  [r"wrong|not right|\bno\b|incorrect|fell|fall|declin|compress|decreas|lower|narrow", r"asset"]),
 ("A bank's cost-to-income ratio improved from 58% to 49% in a year in which its credit impairment charge doubled and it froze all IT spending. The CEO says the bank has clearly become more efficient and more profitable. Give two reasons why that conclusion does not follow.",
  "(1) The cost-to-income ratio is operating expenses / total income and leaves out credit impairments. A doubled impairment charge hits profit, ROA and ROE and shows up in the credit loss ratio, not in the cost-to-income ratio, so the bank can look more efficient while earning less. (2) The cost cut may not last: freezing IT spend (or cutting staff and branches) flatters this year's ratio but can cost income and customers later. Also ask whether income rose because of riskier, higher-yielding lending, which would explain both the better ratio and the higher impairments.",
  r"impairment|credit loss|bad debt|\bclr\b"),
 ("Bank Q reports: LCR 240%, CET1 ratio 19%, loans-to-deposits 52%, cash equal to almost half of its loans, ROA 1.3% and ROE 9% against a cost of equity of 14%. Is this a well-run bank? Diagnose it in terms of the risk-return trade-off.",
  "It is very safe but is destroying value. The LCR is far above 100% and CET1 far above the 12% to 14% SA norm, and only about half of deposits are lent out, so a large share of assets sits in low-yielding liquid assets, and the thick equity base means low leverage. ROE = ROA x leverage: with ROA 1.3% and ROE 9%, leverage is only about 7 times. ROE below the cost of equity means the bank should trade below book value. Liquidity and capital both have an opportunity cost; the bank should lend more or return surplus capital, unless its loan book is unusually risky.",
  [r"liquid|lcr|cash", r"capital|cet1|leverage|equity", r"return|roe|profit|opportunity cost|value|drag"]),
],

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Bank valuation
"b-val": [
 ("A bank's latest reported ROE is 20%. Net income is expected to grow at 6% a year in perpetuity. The risk-free rate is 9.5%, the bank's beta is 1.0 and the market risk premium is 6.5%. Book value per share is R75. What is the fair share price using the implied P/BV (rand, 2 decimals)?",
  "R114.00. ke = 9.5% + 1.0 x 6.5% = 16%. The 20% is historical, so grow it one year: ROE1 = 20% x 1.06 = 21.2%. Implied P/BV = (21.2% - 6%) / (16% - 6%) = 15.2 / 10 = 1.52. Price = 1.52 x R75 = R114.00. Traps: using the ungrown 20% gives 1.40 and R105; subtracting Rf from the 6.5% premium understates ke.", 114),
 ("The risk-free rate is 9%, the expected return on the market is 14.5% and a bank's beta is 0.8. Analysts forecast next year's ROE at 12% and perpetual growth of 4%. What is the implied P/BV (2 decimals)?",
  "0.85. Here the market RETURN is given, so the premium is 14.5% - 9% = 5.5% and ke = 9% + 0.8 x 5.5% = 13.4%. The 12% is already next year's ROE, so do not grow it. P/BV = (12% - 4%) / (13.4% - 4%) = 8 / 9.4 = 0.85. Below 1 because ROE is below ke: the bank is expected to destroy value. If the market P/BV were 0.70, the share would look undervalued on these inputs. Traps: ke = 9% + 0.8 x 14.5% = 20.6%, or growing the forecast ROE to 12.48% (which gives 0.90).", 0.85),
 ("A bank has book equity of R240bn and 1.2bn shares in issue. Next year's ROE is forecast at 17.5% and it pays out 60% of earnings. The cost of equity is 14%. Assuming growth equals the sustainable growth rate, what is the fair price per share (rand)?",
  "R300. g = ROE x (1 - payout) = 17.5% x 0.40 = 7%. Implied P/BV = (17.5% - 7%) / (14% - 7%) = 10.5 / 7 = 1.50. Book value per share = 240 / 1.2 = R200. Price = 1.5 x 200 = R300. Check with the DDM: next dividend = 200 x 17.5% x 60% = R21; 21 / (0.14 - 0.07) = R300. The two methods are the same idea.", 300),
 ("A bank has just paid a dividend of R5.00 per share. Dividends are expected to grow at 10% a year for the next two years and at 5% a year for ever after that. The cost of equity is 13%. What is the share price using the dividend discount model (rand, 2 decimals)?",
  "R71.79. D1 = 5.50, D2 = 6.05, D3 = 6.05 x 1.05 = 6.3525. Terminal value at the end of year 2 = 6.3525 / (0.13 - 0.05) = 79.41. P0 = 5.50 / 1.13 + (6.05 + 79.41) / 1.13^2 = 4.87 + 66.92 = R71.79. The terminal value is about 87% of the price. Traps: capitalising D2 instead of D3, or discounting the terminal value for three years instead of two.", 71.79),
 ("A bank ended last year with CET1 capital (equity) of R61 000m. This year it forecasts net income of R9 000m and has a target payout ratio of 55%. Risk-weighted assets are forecast at R520 000m and the board will not let the CET1 ratio fall below 13% after the dividend. No shares are issued. What dividend can it pay (R million)?",
  "R2 400m. Potential dividend = 9 000 x 55% = 4 950. Available CET1 before the dividend = 61 000 + 9 000 = 70 000. Required CET1 = 13% x 520 000 = 67 600. Excess = 2 400. Dividend = the smaller of 4 950 and 2 400 = R2 400m. The capital constraint binds, so the effective payout is only 26.7% and a DDM built on a 55% payout would overvalue the bank. At the bare 4.5% regulatory minimum the cap (R46 600m) would not bind.", 2400),
 ("An analyst values a bank with a forward ROE of 12%, a payout ratio of 80%, a cost of equity of 10% and perpetual growth of 8%, and gets a P/BV of 2.0. The growth rate is inconsistent with the other inputs. Recalculate the implied P/BV using the growth rate the bank can actually sustain (2 decimals).",
  "1.26. Sustainable growth = ROE x (1 - payout) = 12% x 0.20 = 2.4%, not 8%: a bank that pays out 80% of a 12% ROE cannot grow equity and earnings at 8% without new capital. P/BV = (12% - 2.4%) / (10% - 2.4%) = 9.6 / 7.6 = 1.26, against 4 / 2 = 2.0 with the inflated g. When ROE is above ke, a higher g inflates the value, so g, payout and ROE must fit together.", 1.26),
 ("An analyst values a bank by forecasting free cash flow to the firm, discounting at WACC, and cross-checking with an EV/EBITDA multiple taken from retailers. Give two reasons why this approach breaks down for a bank, and state what should be used instead.",
  "(1) Debt cannot be separated from operations: deposits and borrowings are the bank's raw material, not a financing choice, so interest is a core operating revenue and expense, and EBIT, EBITDA, enterprise value and WACC lose their meaning. (2) Capex and working capital cannot be defined: a bank's investment is growth in its loan book and the regulatory capital that growth needs, so free cash flow to the firm cannot be measured. Use equity methods: the dividend discount model (with dividends capped by CET1), the implied P/BV from ROE, g and ke, or the excess return model, with the market P/BV of comparable banks as a sense check.",
  [r"deposit|debt|interest|raw material|working capital|capex|ebit", r"ddm|dividend|p/?bv?\b|price.to.book|book|excess return|equity"]),
 ("A bank has earned an ROE of 11% against a cost of equity of 14% in each of the last three years, yet its shares trade at a P/BV of 1.6. What does the formula say the P/BV should be relative to 1, what must the market be assuming, and is the share over- or undervalued if ROE does not change?",
  "With ROE below ke the bank is destroying value, so the implied P/BV = (ROE1 - g) / (ke - g) must be below 1 (e.g. with g = 5%: (11.55 - 5) / (14 - 5) = 0.73). A market P/BV of 1.6 means investors are pricing in a recovery: a much higher future ROE, a lower ke, or a takeover. If ROE stays at 11%, the market P/BV is above the implied P/BV, so the share is overvalued. In the write-up compare ROE with ke each year, comment on the gap, then bring in the market P/BV as the market's verdict.",
  [r"below 1|less than 1|under 1|<\s*1|destroy", r"overvalu"]),
],

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Corporate treasury and working capital
"b-treas": [
 ("A wholesaler has sales of R840m (all on credit) and cost of sales of R588m. Receivables were R96m at the start and R120m at the end of the year; inventory R130m and R150m; payables R70m and R84m. Using averages and 365 days, what is the cash conversion cycle (days, 1 decimal)?",
  "86.0 days. Averages: receivables 108, inventory 140, payables 77. DSO = 108 / 840 x 365 = 46.9. DIO = 140 / 588 x 365 = 86.9. DPO = 77 / 588 x 365 = 47.8. CCC = 46.9 + 86.9 - 47.8 = 86.0 days. Traps: DSO uses sales, but DIO and DPO use cost of sales; closing balances give 93.1; adding DPO gives the wrong sign. The operating cycle is 133.8 days.", 86.0),
 ("A wholesaler has sales of R840m and cost of sales of R588m. Average receivables are R108m, average inventory R140m and average payables R77m. It plans to cut DSO to 40 days and DIO to 70 days, but its main supplier will then insist on being paid in 40 days. Using 365 days, what is the net cash released by the whole programme (R million, 2 decimals)?",
  "R30.62m. Target receivables = 840 / 365 x 40 = 92.05, freeing 108 - 92.05 = 15.95. Target inventory = 588 / 365 x 70 = 112.77, freeing 140 - 112.77 = 27.23. Target payables = 588 / 365 x 40 = 64.44, so payables fall by 77 - 64.44 = 12.56, which USES cash. Net = 15.95 + 27.23 - 12.56 = R30.62m. Trap: a lower DPO absorbs cash; adding all three gives R55.74m.", 30.62),
 ("A group has three subsidiaries banking with the same bank, with average balances of +R40m, -R65m and +R55m. The bank pays 4% on credit balances and charges 11% on overdrafts. How much does the group save in net interest per year by putting the three accounts into a notional pool (R million, 2 decimals)?",
  "R4.55m. Without pooling: interest earned 95 x 4% = 3.80; overdraft interest 65 x 11% = 7.15; net cost 3.35. With pooling the bank calculates interest on the net balance of +30: 30 x 4% = 1.20 earned. Saving = 1.20 - (-3.35) = R4.55m, which is the 7% spread on the R65m that is now offset. No money moves, but the bank will want cross-guarantees. A physical sweep gives the same interest result but creates intercompany loans.", 4.55),
 ("A small supplier sells R8m of goods to a large retailer on 90-day terms. The supplier's own bank facility costs 16% a year; under the retailer's supply chain finance programme the bank will pay the approved invoice immediately at a discount based on the retailer's credit, 7.5% a year. Using simple interest and 365 days, how much does the supplier save on this invoice by using the programme instead of its own facility (nearest rand)?",
  "R167 671. Own facility: 8 000 000 x 16% x 90 / 365 = R315 616. SCF: 8 000 000 x 7.5% x 90 / 365 = R147 945. Saving = R167 671 (8.5% x 90 / 365 x R8m). The supplier is paid at once (its DSO falls to about zero) at a cost priced on the BUYER's credit rating; the retailer still pays the bank in full on day 90, so its DPO is unchanged or longer. It is not a loan to the supplier.", 167671),
 ("A manufacturer earns net profit of R54m on total assets of R540m. Cost of sales is R438m a year. It cuts DIO from 90 to 60 days and uses the cash released to repay a loan costing 10% a year before tax (tax rate 27%). Using 365 days and assuming nothing else changes, what is the new ROA (percent, 2 decimals)?",
  "11.24%. Cash freed = 30 / 365 x 438 = R36m. Interest saved = 36 x 10% = 3.6 before tax, 3.6 x 0.73 = 2.628 after tax, so profit = 56.628. Assets fall to 540 - 36 = 504. ROA = 56.628 / 504 = 11.24%, up from 10.00%. Lower working capital raises ROA through both a smaller asset base and a lower financing cost (and raises FCFF through a lower change in NWC), at the price of a higher stock-out risk.", 11.24),
 ("A large retailer with a cost of capital of 8% lengthens payment terms to a small supplier from 45 to 90 days; the supplier's cost of capital is 18%. The retailer's treasurer says this 'creates' working capital for the supply chain. Why is that wrong, how is the supplier likely to respond, and what arrangement gives the retailer its longer DPO without the damage?",
  "It only moves cash: the retailer's higher DPO is the supplier's higher DSO, so the chain's total cash cycle does not shorten, and the funding burden shifts to the party with the HIGHER cost of capital (18% instead of 8%), so the chain as a whole is worse off. The supplier passes the cost back through higher prices, or its service and solvency suffer. Use supply chain finance (reverse factoring): the bank pays the supplier early on the approved invoice at a rate based on the retailer's credit, and the retailer pays the bank on day 90. Only a lower DIO releases cash for the whole chain.",
  [r"supply chain financ|reverse factoring|\bscf\b", r"price|cost of capital|dso|shift|mov|pass|transfer"]),
 ("Firm A has a current ratio of 1.6 and a cash conversion cycle of 75 days. Firm B, a grocery retailer, has a current ratio of 0.9 and a CCC of -5 days. A credit analyst concludes that A is the more liquid business. Explain why B may actually be in the stronger cash position.",
  "The current ratio is a static measure of amounts at one date and ignores the timing of cash flows; much of A's current assets may be slow inventory and debtors, which is exactly why its ratio is high. B sells for cash and turns stock quickly but pays suppliers later, so it collects from customers 5 days before it pays suppliers: suppliers finance its operations and it needs no funding for the cycle. A must fund 75 days of working capital itself. This is the Clicks versus Dis-Chem lesson: judge liquidity by the cash cycle, not only by the ratios. B's risk is that its position depends on its bargaining power over suppliers.",
  r"timing|cycle|\bccc\b|supplier|static|snapshot|one date|point in time"),
 ("A group has subsidiaries in South Africa (using three different banks), China and Germany. The treasurer wants every account worldwide swept to zero each night into one rand master account in Johannesburg. Give two problems with this design and describe the structure the course suggests instead.",
  "Problems: (1) a physical sweep moves real money between legal entities, so it creates intercompany loans, which some countries (China) tax; (2) true end-of-day sweeping needs one bank: across several banks and time zones, execution delays leave balances behind; (3) cross-currency sweeps are costly and add exchange-rate risk. Suggested structure: two-stage sweeping, first into a master account per country (and per currency), then link the currency pools by notional pooling, where no money moves and the bank only nets balances for interest. Notional pooling in turn needs joint cross-guarantees and usually one bank in one jurisdiction, so the group may first have to consolidate its banking relationships.",
  [r"intercompany loan|tax|time zone|currenc|several banks|one bank", r"two.stage|notional|per country|by currency|regional"]),
],

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Multinational treasury
"b-mnc": [
 ("Four group companies owe each other the following in a month (R million; payer -> receiver): SA -> UK 12, SA -> Kenya 5, SA -> India 8; UK -> SA 9, UK -> Kenya 6, UK -> India 4; Kenya -> SA 7, Kenya -> UK 3, Kenya -> India 2; India -> SA 10, India -> UK 11, India -> Kenya 5. Transfer and currency-conversion costs are 0.5% of every rand moved. How much does multilateral netting save compared with settling every payment gross (rand)?",
  "R350 000. Gross payments total 82 (SA pays 25, UK 19, Kenya 12, India 26). Net positions (received - paid): SA 26 - 25 = +1; UK 26 - 19 = +7; Kenya 16 - 12 = +4; India 14 - 26 = -12. Only India pays: 1 to SA, 7 to UK and 4 to Kenya, so 12 moves in 3 payments instead of 82 in 12. Saving = (82 - 12) x 0.5% = R0.35m. Check: the net positions sum to zero. Bilateral netting alone would still move 20.", 350000),
 ("Trident SA is owed BRL 10m by its own Brazilian subsidiary, due in 90 days. The spot rate is R3.40 per real and treasury expects R3.10 in 90 days. If the subsidiary pays today, Trident can invest the rand at 8% a year (simple interest, 365 days). What is the expected gain, measured in rand at day 90, from leading the receipt instead of waiting (nearest rand)?",
  "R3 670 685. Lead: BRL 10m x 3.40 = R34 000 000 today, invested for 90 days: 34 000 000 x (1 + 0.08 x 90 / 365) = R34 670 685. Lag: BRL 10m x 3.10 = R31 000 000. Expected gain = R3 670 685. The real is the soft currency, so a receivable in it should be led (collected early). Because it is an intrafirm balance the group can simply change the credit terms. Caveats: the gain is only expected, the subsidiary loses the use of the cash (compare interest rates in Brazil), and exchange controls may limit early payment.", 3670685),
 ("Trident SA must pay a German supplier EUR 1.5m in six months. Spot is R20.00 per euro, the six-month forward rate is R20.60 and treasury's own forecast for six months' time is R21.20. Trident could (a) pay today, borrowing the rand at 10% a year for half a year, (b) buy the euros forward, or (c) wait and buy at the future spot rate. Measured in rand at the six-month date, what is the cost of the cheapest alternative (R million, 2 decimals)?",
  "R30.90m, the forward. (a) Lead: 1.5m x 20.00 = R30.0m today plus interest of 30.0 x 10% x 0.5 = 1.5, so R31.50m. (b) Forward: 1.5m x 20.60 = R30.90m, certain. (c) Wait: 1.5m x 21.20 = R31.80m, and only a forecast. Trap: leading looks R1.8m cheaper than waiting until the funding cost is included; then it saves only R0.3m and is beaten by the forward. Leading a hard-currency payable is right in direction, but the interest cost must be weighed against the expected currency move.", 30.9),
 ("Trident quotes a US customer US$800 000 when the rate is R18.00. The order is confirmed at R18.30, the goods are shipped and invoiced at R18.60, and the customer pays when the rate is R17.90. What foreign exchange gain or loss does Trident record on settling the receivable (rand; negative for a loss)?",
  "-R560 000. The sale is recorded at the spot rate on the shipment date: 800 000 x 18.60 = R14 880 000. Cash received = 800 000 x 17.90 = R14 320 000. FX loss = (17.90 - 18.60) x 800 000 = -R560 000. Trap: the quote rate of R18.00 is not the accounting base, but the transaction exposure did begin at the quote (quotation exposure), ran through backlog exposure from the order, and became billing exposure at shipment. Against the rate it priced at, Trident is R80 000 worse off.", -560000),
 ("Trident Europe has surplus cash of R200m and Trident SA needs to borrow R150m for the year. Outside banks pay 5% on deposits and charge 10.5% on loans. Trident's in-house bank pays 6.5% on deposits and charges 9% on loans, and places any cash it cannot lend internally with an outside bank at 5%. By how much is the group better off per year using the in-house bank (R million, 2 decimals)?",
  "R8.25m. Outside banks: earn 200 x 5% = 10.00, pay 150 x 10.5% = 15.75, net cost 5.75. In-house bank: the only outside flow is 50 x 5% = 2.50 earned on the unlent surplus, a net gain of 2.50. Benefit = 2.50 - (-5.75) = R8.25m, which is the outside spread of 5.5% on the R150m now funded internally. It is shared: Europe earns 3.0 more, SA pays 2.25 less, and the in-house bank keeps 150 x 9% + 50 x 5% - 200 x 6.5% = 3.0. Trap: the internal rates only divide the gain; they do not change its size.", 8.25),
 ("A foreign subsidiary earns R100m before tax and before any payments to its parent. The host country taxes company profits at 30% and levies a 10% withholding tax on dividends; royalties are tax-deductible there and carry no withholding tax. Compare (a) remitting all after-tax profit as a dividend with (b) first charging a R40m royalty and then remitting the remaining after-tax profit as a dividend. Ignoring home-country tax, how much more cash reaches the parent under (b) (R million, 1 decimal)?",
  "R14.8m. (a) Tax 30, dividend 70, withholding tax 7: the parent receives 63.0. (b) The royalty of 40 arrives untaxed; taxable profit 60, tax 18, dividend 42, withholding tax 4.2, so 37.8; total 77.8. Difference = 14.8, which is the 37% tax leakage avoided on R40m. This is unbundling: royalties, fees and transfer prices are paid before tax, while dividends are paid after tax and are the most tax-inefficient conduit. The royalty must be a fair charge for real intellectual property, or the host tax authority will challenge it.", 14.8),
 ("A profitable Trident subsidiary in a country suffering a 'dollar drought' holds local currency worth US$30m that it wants to pay to the parent as a dividend, but local banks cannot supply dollars and the currency is losing about 2% a month. Which constraint on repositioning funds is this, what two further risks does the trapped cash create, and name two techniques the group can use to reduce the balance exposed.",
  "It is a liquidity constraint (blocked or trapped funds): the profit exists but hard currency is not available to convert it; if the cause is formal exchange controls or an inconvertible currency it is overt political blockage. Further risks: currency depreciation while the cash waits (it buys fewer dollars every month) and import constraints on the subsidiary itself, on top of the repatriation risk. Techniques: lead payments out of the soft currency (pay intrafirm payables, fees and royalties early); unbundle remittances into royalties, management fees and transfer prices instead of one large dividend; net the balance against what the group owes the subsidiary; or use the cash locally (pay local suppliers, buy inventory or assets, reinvest).",
  [r"liquidity|block|overt|inconvertib|trapped|repatriat", r"royalt|fee|transfer pric|lead|net|unbundl|reinvest|intrafirm|local"]),
 ("A South African exporter has won its first order, worth EUR 2m, from an unknown buyer in a country where foreign firms rarely win court cases. The buyer wants the goods shipped first with payment in 90 days; the exporter wants cash before shipping. Which instrument resolves the deadlock, how do the related documents protect each side, and when did the exporter's currency exposure begin?",
  "A letter of credit: the buyer's bank promises to pay the exporter on presentation of specified documents, so the bank's credit replaces the unknown buyer's. The exporter ships with title passing on an order bill of lading (receipt, contract of carriage and document of title) and claims payment with a draft (a sight draft for immediate payment, or a time draft for 90 days that it can discount). The buyer is protected because the bank pays only against documents proving shipment; the exporter is protected because payment no longer depends on the buyer or its courts. The FX exposure began when the euro price was quoted (quotation exposure), not at invoicing. Because the documents fix timing and amount, a hedge can be matched and banks will finance the trade.",
  [r"letter of credit|l/c\b|\blc\b", r"bill of lading|draft|document|title"]),
],

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Banks, money creation and monetary policy
"b-money": [
 ("A bank pledges a government bond with a market value of R250m and 12 years to maturity in a 7-day repo with the SARB. Haircut factors: Treasury bills 1.010; bonds of 1 to 10 years 1.025; bonds over 10 years 1.035. The repo rate is 7.25% NACM (a month is 30 days). How much interest does the SARB earn on the transaction (nearest rand)?",
  "R340 033. Adjusted value = 250 000 000 / 1.035 = R241 545 894. Interest factor = 1 + (0.0725 / 12) x (7 / 30) = 1.00140972. Maximum liquidity X = 241 545 894 / 1.00140972 = R241 205 860. Interest = 241 545 894 - 241 205 860 = R340 033 (about R48 576 a day). Traps: using the 1.025 factor (the residual maturity is over 10 years); multiplying by the factor instead of dividing; computing interest on R250m.", 340033),
 ("The SARB policy rate is 6.75%. A bank has total liabilities of R480bn and holds R37bn in total at the SARB. Its quota is R18bn. It lends everything above the quota to another bank overnight at 6.50% throughout the year instead of leaving it at the SARB. What is its total annual interest income on these balances (R million, 1 decimal)?",
  "R1 670.0m. Required reserves = 2.5% x 480 = R12bn, earning 0%. Surplus = 37 - 12 = R25bn. Up to quota: 18 x 6.75% = R1 215m. Above quota: R7bn lent in the interbank market at 6.50% = R455m (left at the SARB it would earn only SPR - 1% = 5.75%, i.e. R402.5m). Total = R1 670m, R52.5m more than doing nothing. The borrower gains too, since an SARB overdraft costs SPR + 1% = 7.75%. That is what the quota is designed to encourage.", 1670),
 ("Under the textbook fractional-reserve model the reserve ratio is 12.5% and the central bank injects R2 000 of new reserves, deposited at Bank A. Each bank keeps the required fraction and lends the rest, which is redeposited at the next bank. What is the total of new loans made by the first three banks, A, B and C (rand, 2 decimals)?",
  "R4 621.09. A lends 2 000 x 0.875 = 1 750.00; B lends 1 750 x 0.875 = 1 531.25; C lends 1 531.25 x 0.875 = 1 339.84; total 4 621.09. In the limit, deposits reach 2 000 / 0.125 = R16 000 (multiplier 8) and loans R14 000. The course treats this model as outdated: banks lend first and find reserves afterwards, the central bank supplies reserves on demand at its policy rate, and countries with no reserve requirement still have bank lending.", 4621.09),
 ("Bank A has deposit liabilities of R2 000m and holds R60m of reserves at the SARB. It grants a R500m loan by crediting the borrower's account. The borrower then pays R400m to suppliers who bank with Bank B. After the payment, how much in reserves must Bank A obtain (from the interbank market or an SARB repo) to settle the payment and hold the 2.5% minimum against its deposits? Ignore any requirement on the new borrowing itself. Answer in R million, 1 decimal.",
  "R392.5m. Granting the loan: loans +500 and deposits +500 (to 2 500); no reserves are needed to create the money. Settlement: R400m of reserves must move to Bank B, so Bank A's reserves would be 60 - 400 = -340, and its deposits are 2 100. Required reserves = 2.5% x 2 100 = 52.5. Reserves to obtain = 340 + 52.5 = R392.5m. Lesson: one bank creates money by lending; reserves matter for settlement, and the bank finds them afterwards at a price set by the policy rate.", 392.5),
 ("The SARB policy rate is 7%. A bank holds R3.0bn of banknotes in vaults and ATMs but needs only R1.8bn for daily operations. It moves the excess to its account at the SARB, where R0.5bn fits under its quota and the rest is above quota. Its required reserves are already met. How much extra interest does the bank earn per year (R million)?",
  "R77m. Excess notes = 3.0 - 1.8 = R1.2bn, earning nothing in the vault. At the SARB: 0.5bn x 7% = R35m, plus 0.7bn x (7% - 1%) = R42m; total R77m. Trap: applying 7% to the full R1.2bn gives R84m; only surplus within the quota earns the full policy rate. This is why banks have economised on physical cash since 2022: notes and required reserves earn 0%, surplus reserves earn the policy rate.", 77),
 ("In an SARB repo a Treasury bill carries a haircut factor of 1.010 but a 15-year government bond carries 1.035. Explain what risk the haircut protects the SARB against, and why the long bond needs the bigger one.",
  "The haircut protects against market risk on the collateral: if the bank fails to repay, the SARB must sell the security, and its price may have fallen in the meantime, leaving the loan under-secured. (Credit risk is the bank not repaying; the haircut covers the collateral losing value.) A longer-maturity bond has a longer duration, so its price is more sensitive to a change in interest rates and can fall further during the few days of the loan. A bigger margin is therefore needed to keep the loan fully covered.",
  [r"market|price|value", r"duration|sensitiv|longer|maturit"]),
 ("A bank's credit committee minutes say: 'We cannot approve new loans this month because deposits have been flat, and with a 2.5% reserve ratio the system can only lend 40 times the reserves the SARB gives us.' Identify the two errors about money creation, and state what actually limits the bank's lending.",
  "Error 1: banks do not lend out existing deposits. A loan is made by crediting the borrower's account, so the loan creates the deposit (credit creation theory); savings are the result of lending, not its source. Error 2: the money multiplier does not control lending. The SARB sets the price of reserves (the policy rate), not the quantity; banks lend first and obtain reserves afterwards for settlement. The real limits are indirect: finding creditworthy borrowers and loan demand, the cost of funding and reserves, capital and liquidity requirements (measured after the fact), and confidence.",
  [r"loan.{0,40}creat|creat.{0,40}deposit|credit creation", r"capital|creditworth|borrower|demand|policy rate|price|cost|liquidity|confidence"]),
 ("Since June 2022 SA banks hold large surplus reserves and almost never borrow from the SARB through repos. A student argues: 'So a 50 basis point increase in the policy rate no longer affects banks, because they are not paying that rate on anything.' Explain why the student is wrong and how the increase still reaches borrowers.",
  "In the surplus (tiered-floor) system the policy rate is mainly a deposit rate: it is what a bank earns risk-free on surplus reserves up to its quota. It works through opportunity cost: no bank will lend to anyone for less than it can earn at the SARB, so the policy rate is the floor under interbank and other short-term rates. When it rises by 50 basis points, interbank rates, deposit rates and prime (policy rate + 3.5 points) rise with it, variable-rate borrowers pay more, fewer loans are granted, less new money is created and demand cools. Under the old shortage system the same rate worked as the cost of borrowing from the SARB.",
  r"floor|deposit rate|opportunity cost|earn|surplus"),
],

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Bank regulation
"b-reg": [
 ("Bank Z (R million): total equity 96 000, which includes preference shares of 4 000, AT1 instruments of 6 000 and a positive cash-flow hedging reserve of 1 500. Assets include goodwill 5 200, capitalised software 3 300 and a deferred tax asset of 2 000, of which 70% depends on future taxable income. The regulator judges that loan provisions were too aggressive by 1 100. Liabilities include Basel III compliant subordinated debt of 8 000. RWA are 610 000. What is the CET1 ratio (percent, 2 decimals)?",
  "12.41%. Tier 1 = 96 000 - 5 200 - 3 300 - 1 400 (70% of the DTA) - 1 500 + 1 100 (provision add-back) = 85 700. CET1 = 85 700 - 4 000 - 6 000 = 75 700. CET1 ratio = 75 700 / 610 000 = 12.41%. For reference: Tier 1 ratio 14.05%; total capital = 85 700 + 8 000 = 93 700, ratio 15.36%. Traps: leaving preference shares and AT1 in CET1 (that is the Tier 1 ratio); adding the subordinated debt to CET1; deducting the whole DTA; forgetting that a positive hedging reserve is deducted.", 12.41),
 ("Bank Z has CET1 capital of R75 700m, total capital of R93 700m and RWA of R610 000m. Its cash and balances at the SARB total R21 000m and its total liabilities are R760 000m. It must keep a CET1 ratio of at least 11.5%, a total capital ratio of at least 15% and a cash reserve ratio of at least 2.5%. What is the largest cash dividend it can pay (R million)?",
  "R2 000m. A cash dividend reduces CET1, total capital and cash by the same amount; RWA (cash has a 0% weight) and liabilities do not change. CET1 headroom = 75 700 - 11.5% x 610 000 = 75 700 - 70 150 = 5 550. Total capital headroom = 93 700 - 15% x 610 000 = 93 700 - 91 500 = 2 200. Cash headroom = 21 000 - 2.5% x 760 000 = 21 000 - 19 000 = 2 000. The smallest headroom binds: R2 000m, set by the liquidity test, not the capital tests. Always test every minimum.", 2000),
 ("A D-SIB holds (R million): government bonds and SARB balances 90 000 (0% weight); interbank claims 40 000 (20%); residential mortgages 260 000 (50%); corporate loans 180 000 (100%); non-performing loans 20 000 (150%); goodwill 5 000 (already deducted from capital). It also has undrawn credit commitments of 60 000 with a 50% credit conversion factor and a 100% weight. Its required total capital ratio is 8% plus the 2.5% conservation buffer plus a 1% D-SIB surcharge. It holds total capital of 41 000. What is its capital shortfall (R million)?",
  "R2 470m. RWA = 0 + 8 000 + 130 000 + 180 000 + 30 000 + (60 000 x 50% x 100% = 30 000) = 378 000. Goodwill is left out of RWA because it has been deducted from capital. Required ratio = 8% + 2.5% + 1% = 11.5%; required capital = 11.5% x 378 000 = 43 470. Shortfall = 43 470 - 41 000 = 2 470 (the actual ratio is 10.85%). Traps: ignoring the off-balance-sheet commitments (the bank would then seem to pass), or giving goodwill a risk weight.", 2470),
 ("A bank holds (R million): vault cash 6 000; balance at the SARB 14 000; Level 1 government bonds 30 000, of which 8 000 are pledged as collateral in a repo; Level 2A corporate bonds 10 000 (15% haircut); and a committed SARB facility of 3 000. In a 30-day stress it assumes run-off of 5% of retail deposits of 400 000 and 40% of wholesale deposits of 150 000, and drawdown of 10% of undrawn commitments of 50 000. Stressed inflows are 30 000. What is the LCR (percent, 2 decimals)?",
  "97.27%. HQLA = 6 000 + 14 000 + (30 000 - 8 000 encumbered) + 10 000 x 0.85 + 3 000 = 53 500. Outflows = 20 000 + 60 000 + 5 000 = 85 000; net outflows = 85 000 - 30 000 = 55 000 (inflows are well inside the 75% cap). LCR = 53 500 / 55 000 = 97.27%, below 100%: it needs R1 500m more HQLA. Traps: counting the pledged bonds (HQLA must be unencumbered), forgetting the haircut, or inverting the ratio. Note that wholesale deposits are 27% of deposits but 71% of the outflows.", 97.27),
 ("A bank is funded by (R million) equity 60 000, debt maturing after more than one year 40 000, retail deposits 150 000 and short-term wholesale funding 350 000. ASF factors: equity and long-term debt 100%, retail deposits 90%, short-term wholesale 30%. Its assets and RSF factors: cash and reserves 30 000 (0%), government bonds 70 000 (5%), mortgages 200 000 (65%), other loans 280 000 (85%), fixed assets 20 000 (100%). How much short-term wholesale funding must it replace with debt of more than one year to bring the NSFR to exactly 100% (R million, nearest whole number)?",
  "R73 571m. ASF = 60 000 + 40 000 + 135 000 + 105 000 = 340 000. RSF = 0 + 3 500 + 130 000 + 238 000 + 20 000 = 391 500. NSFR = 340 000 / 391 500 = 86.8%, below 100%: a shortfall of 51 500 of stable funding. Each rand switched from wholesale (30%) to long-term debt (100%) adds only 0.70 of ASF, so 51 500 / 0.70 = 73 571. Trap: switching only 51 500 leaves the bank short, because the wholesale funding it replaces was already counted at 30%.", 73571),
 ("Mrs Dube has a savings account of R80 000 and a fixed deposit of R70 000 at Bank X, and a savings account of R60 000 at Bank Y. Her employer's pension fund has R5m on deposit at Bank X. Both banks fail. How much in total does CODI pay Mrs Dube (rand)?",
  "R160 000. Cover is R100 000 per qualifying depositor PER BANK, with all accounts at the same bank added together. Bank X: 80 000 + 70 000 = 150 000, capped at 100 000 (the other 50 000 is a claim on the failed bank's estate). Bank Y: 60 000, fully covered. Total 160 000. The pension fund gets nothing from CODI: institutional and wholesale depositors are excluded so that they keep monitoring banks (market discipline). Traps: one R100 000 limit across both banks (100 000), or a separate limit per account (210 000).", 160000),
 ("A bank has a CET1 ratio of 18% and an LCR of 130%, but it funds a book of 20-year mortgages almost entirely with 60-day wholesale deposits that it rolls over. The board says its strong capital and LCR make it safe. Which Basel III ratio is aimed at this weakness, over what horizon, why do capital and the LCR not deal with it, and which failure illustrates it?",
  "The net stable funding ratio (NSFR): available stable funding / required stable funding of at least 100% over a one-year horizon. Long-term, illiquid assets must be funded by stable sources (equity, long-term debt, sticky retail deposits), and short-term wholesale funding counts for little. Capital protects against losses (solvency), not against funding that fails to roll over, and the LCR only covers 30 days of stress. A solvent bank can still fail if its wholesale funders walk away: Northern Rock (2007) and Saambou (2002). The fix is structural: lengthen the funding or change the asset mix.",
  [r"nsfr|net stable funding", r"liquid|funding|roll|maturity|wholesale|solven"]),
 ("Two banks each have R1 000bn of assets and R35bn of Tier 1 capital. Bank A holds mainly corporate loans (RWA R500bn). Bank B has used its internal models to assign very low risk weights to a large portfolio of foreign government bonds and reports RWA of only R250bn. Compare their Tier 1 ratios and their leverage ratios, and explain which measure exposes Bank B and why Basel III introduced it.",
  "Tier 1 ratios: A = 35 / 500 = 7%; B = 35 / 250 = 14%, so B looks twice as strong. Leverage ratios (Tier 1 / exposure, no risk weights): both 35 / 1 000 = 3.5%, only just above the 3% minimum. The leverage ratio exposes B: its apparent strength comes entirely from the risk weights it chose, not from more capital. Basel III added the leverage ratio as a simple backstop that cannot be gamed through risk weights or internal models; the course example is European banks giving Greek government bonds a 0% weight shortly before they lost value. However low the weights, assets cannot exceed about 33 times Tier 1.",
  [r"leverage", r"risk.weight|unweighted|gam|model|backstop"]),
],

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Bank failures
"b-fail": [
 ("A bank has cash of R40m and loans of R500m, funded by deposits of R480m and equity of R60m. In a run it pays withdrawals first from cash and then by selling loans, which fetch only 75 cents in the rand. What is the largest total withdrawal it can meet before its equity is wiped out (R million)?",
  "R220m. The first R40m comes from cash at no loss. To raise each further rand it must sell loans with a book value of 1 / 0.75 = R1.33, a loss of R0.33 per rand raised. Equity of 60 is exhausted when x / 3 = 60, so x = R180m raised from loan sales (book value 240, loss 60). Total = 40 + 180 = R220m, only 46% of deposits. A solvent bank (assets 540 against deposits 480) is made insolvent by a pure liquidity run; only a lender of last resort lending against the loans, so that they need not be sold, breaks the chain.", 220),
 ("Two depositors each have R200 in a bank. If both wait, each receives R280. If one withdraws early she gets her R200 and the other is left with R100. If both withdraw early they share the R300 liquidation value, R150 each. Depositor A believes there is a probability p that B will withdraw early. Above what value of p does A do better by withdrawing (percent, 2 decimals)?",
  "61.54%. Expected payoff from waiting = 280(1 - p) + 100p = 280 - 180p. From withdrawing = 200(1 - p) + 150p = 200 - 50p. Equal when 80 = 130p, so p = 0.6154. If A thinks a run is more likely than that, running is her rational choice even though the bank is sound. There are two Nash equilibria, (wait, wait) and (withdraw, withdraw); which one occurs depends on expectations, not on solvency. Deposit insurance and a credible lender of last resort work by keeping p low.", 61.54),
 ("A bank holds R90bn of fixed-rate government bonds with a modified duration of 5.6, classified as held to maturity and funded by demand deposits. Its book equity is R15bn. Market yields rise by 3.5 percentage points and the bank has no hedges. What is the bank's equity if the bonds are marked to market (R billion, 2 decimals; negative if insolvent)?",
  "-R2.64bn. Fall in bond value = 5.6 x 90 x 3.5% = R17.64bn. Equity = 15 - 17.64 = -2.64: economically insolvent. The loss stays hidden while the bonds are carried at cost, but becomes real the moment deposits leave and bonds must be sold. This is Silicon Valley Bank: interest rate risk (long-duration assets funded by demand deposits, unhedged) plus concentrated, uninsured depositors who ran within a day. Credit risk played no part; the bonds were 'safe'.", -2.64),
 ("A failing bank's assets are written down from R100bn to R76bn. Its funding is: retail deposits R32bn, senior bonds R50bn, subordinated (Tier 2) debt R5bn, AT1 instruments R3bn and ordinary equity R10bn. The resolution authority protects depositors in full and bails in the other claims in the normal order of loss absorption. What haircut do senior bondholders take (percent)?",
  "12%. Loss = 100 - 76 = R24bn. Order of absorption: ordinary equity 10, then AT1 3, then Tier 2 debt 5, which together absorb 18. The remaining 6 falls on senior bonds: 6 / 50 = 12%, so they receive 88 cents in the rand. Compare African Bank (2014): shareholders wiped out, subordinated debt written down, senior bondholders took a 10% haircut, depositors safe. Credit Suisse (2023) inverted the order: CHF 16bn of AT1 was written to zero while shareholders still received UBS shares.", 12),
 ("Bank P's assets are worth R110bn against liabilities of R100bn, but 60% of its funding is 30-day wholesale deposits and a rumour has started a run. Bank Q meets every withdrawal comfortably from a stream of new municipal deposits, but a third of its loan book is fictitious. For each bank state whether it is solvent and whether it is liquid, name the SA failure it resembles, and give the appropriate official response.",
  "Bank P is solvent but illiquid, like Saambou (2002). The right response is lender-of-last-resort support (emergency liquidity assistance against good collateral, Bagehot's rule) so that it need not sell assets at fire-sale prices; refusing it was the regulatory error in Saambou. Bank Q is insolvent but still liquid, like VBS: solvency was lost first through fraud, and the liquidity crisis comes when the new deposits stop. Liquidity support would be a bail-out of an insolvent bank; it needs resolution (formerly curatorship), with bail-in or liquidation, CODI paying covered depositors, and prosecution. Liquidity trouble is the visible symptom; the cause lies in funding structure, asset quality or governance.",
  [r"lender.of.last.resort|\bllr\b|\bela\b|emergency liquidity|liquidity (support|assistance)", r"resol|bail.?in|curator|liquidat|capital"]),
 ("A small SA bank fails in 2026. A retail depositor has R250 000 with it. A journalist writes that 'the Minister will appoint a curator whose job is to nurse the bank back to health, and depositors will have to wait and hope'. Correct the journalist on the regime, the authority, the primary objective and what happens to this depositor.",
  "Since 1 June 2023 failures are handled under resolution, not curatorship. The SARB is the resolution authority and appoints a resolution practitioner. The primary objective is to safeguard financial stability, sacrificing the institution if necessary, not to save the individual bank. The authority has statutory powers to bail in creditors, set up a bridge bank, split good bank and bad bank or transfer assets without shareholder consent. The depositor is paid R100 000 by CODI (funded by bank levies, not taxpayers); the other R150 000 is a claim on the failed bank's estate, recovered only as its loans are collected. Ditsobotla (2025) was the first case.",
  [r"resolution", r"100\s?000|codi"]),
 ("The Governor announces on television that a bank is sound, and a depositor with R60 000 in it (fully covered by deposit insurance) still queues to withdraw. A commentator calls her irrational. Give two reasons from the course why her behaviour can be rational.",
  "(1) Sequential service: deposits are paid first come, first served, so if she expects others to run, the best response is to run too, whatever she believes about the bank's solvency (the bank-run equilibrium of Diamond and Dybvig). (2) Insurance and assurances protect the amount, not access: in a curatorship or resolution her money may be frozen for weeks or months (Saambou depositors waited up to six months). (3) The costs are asymmetric: withdrawing costs a queue or a few taps on an app, while staying risks losing access to her savings. Northern Rock's run continued after a full government guarantee. Individually rational choices produce a collectively bad outcome (a coordination failure).",
  r"access|frozen|froze|sequential|first.come|queue|cheap|cost(s)? (little|nothing)|delay|wait|expect"),
 ("A regional bank has 92% of its deposits above the insurance limit, nearly all from venture-backed technology firms that follow the same few investors, and it has invested those deposits in 10-year fixed-rate government bonds with no hedges. Interest rates rise by 4 percentage points. Identify the two separate weaknesses, explain how they combine into a failure within days, and name the real case.",
  "Asset side: interest rate risk. Long-duration fixed-rate bonds funded by demand deposits lose a large part of their value when rates rise (duration x change in yield), and the loss was unhedged and hidden by held-to-maturity accounting. Liability side: concentrated, uninsured funding: one industry, depositors who all know each other and all have reason to run because they are above the insurance limit. When the bank sells bonds at a loss to meet outflows, the loss is revealed, uninsured depositors run using their phones, and more bonds must be sold at a loss: illiquidity turns into insolvency. This is Silicon Valley Bank (2023), which faced about $42bn of withdrawals in one day. Diversification matters on the liability side too.",
  [r"interest.rate|duration|bond", r"uninsured|concentrat|deposit|funding"]),
],

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Risk overview, RAROC and operational risk
"b-risk": [
 ("A bank is asked for a R40m loan with a duration of 3.27 years. The average rate on bonds in the borrower's rating class is 9%, and the largest increase in the class's credit premium over the past year at the 99% level was 1.8%. The projected spread is 0.45% and fees are 0.30% of the loan. The risk-free rate is 8%, the bank's beta is 1.2 and the market risk premium is 5.5%. Calculate the RAROC (percent, 2 decimals).",
  "13.89%. D* = 3.27 / 1.09 = 3.0. Capital at risk = 3.0 x 40 000 000 x 0.018 = R2 160 000. One-year net income = (0.45% + 0.30%) x 40m = R300 000. RAROC = 300 000 / 2 160 000 = 13.89%. Hurdle = cost of equity = 8% + 1.2 x 5.5% = 14.6%. RAROC is below the hurdle, so do not approve on these terms: raise the spread or fees, or shorten the loan. Traps: using the duration of 3.27 without dividing by 1.09; subtracting Rf from the premium.", 13.89),
 ("A R25m loan has a duration of 4.4 years; the average rate on bonds in its rating class is 10% and the worst-case rise in the loan rate is 1.5%. The projected spread is 0.60%. The bank's hurdle rate is 16%. What is the minimum fee, as a percentage of the loan, that makes the loan acceptable (percent, 2 decimals)?",
  "0.36%. D* = 4.4 / 1.10 = 4.0. Capital at risk = 4.0 x 25 000 000 x 0.015 = R1 500 000. Required net income = 16% x 1 500 000 = R240 000. Spread income = 0.60% x 25m = R150 000. Fees needed = R90 000 = 0.36% of R25m. At that fee RAROC is exactly 16%. This is RAROC used as a pricing tool: if the loan falls short of the hurdle, adjust the fee, the premium or the duration instead of simply refusing.", 0.36),
 ("A R60m one-year loan is priced at a base rate of 11% plus a 2.5% risk premium, with a 0.25% origination fee and an 8% compensating balance; the reserve requirement is 2.5%. The bank's cost of funds is 10.5% on the funds it actually provides. The borrower's PD is 1.5% with an LGD of 40%. The loan's modified duration is 2.8 and the worst-case rate change is 3%. Deducting the expected loss from income, what is the RAROC (percent, 2 decimals)?",
  "41.30%. Gross income = 13.5% x 60 + 0.25% x 60 = 8.10 + 0.15 = R8.25m. Net funds provided = 60 x [1 - 0.08 x (1 - 0.025)] = 60 x 0.922 = R55.32m; funding cost = 10.5% x 55.32 = R5.8086m. Expected loss = 1.5% x 40% x 60 = R0.36m. Net income = 8.25 - 5.8086 - 0.36 = R2.0814m. Capital at risk = 2.8 x 60 x 0.03 = R5.04m. RAROC = 2.0814 / 5.04 = 41.30%. Expected loss is a cost in the numerator (covered by pricing); unexpected loss is the capital in the denominator. Trap: 2.8 is already a modified duration, so do not divide by (1 + R) again.", 41.3),
 ("A bank has three exposures. Loan A: R50m drawn, PD 2%, collateral expected to recover 70%. Loan B: R30m drawn, PD 5%, recovery 40%. Facility C: a R40m limit of which R10m is drawn; the bank expects half of the undrawn amount to be drawn by the time of default; PD 4%, LGD 60%. What is the total expected loss (R million, 2 decimals)?",
  "R1.80m. EL = PD x LGD x EAD. A: 2% x (1 - 0.70) x 50 = 0.30. B: 5% x (1 - 0.40) x 30 = 0.90. C: EAD = 10 + 50% x 30 = 25; 4% x 60% x 25 = 0.60. Total = R1.80m. Traps: using the recovery rate as the LGD (LGD = 1 - recovery), and using only the R10m drawn (or the full R40m limit) for C. This R1.8m is covered by the risk premium in the loan rates and by provisions; capital is held for losses above it.", 1.8),
 ("Two R10m loans are on offer and the bank can fund only one. Loan X: spread plus fees 1.2%, duration 1.62 years, worst-case rate change 2.0%. Loan Y: spread plus fees 2.0%, duration 5.4 years, worst-case rate change 2.5%. The average rate on bonds in both rating classes is 8%. What is the RAROC of the loan the bank should prefer (percent, 1 decimal)?",
  "40.0% (Loan X). X: D* = 1.62 / 1.08 = 1.5; capital at risk = 1.5 x 10m x 0.02 = R300 000; income R120 000; RAROC = 40.0%. Y: D* = 5.4 / 1.08 = 5.0; capital at risk = 5.0 x 10m x 0.025 = R1 250 000; income R200 000; RAROC = 16.0%. Trap: Y earns more in rand and has the higher return per rand lent, but it ties up over four times the capital. RAROC compares loans of different risk on a like-for-like basis, which the promised return per rand lent cannot do.", 40.0),
 ("Classify each event under the Basel definition of operational risk: (a) a trader hides losses in an unauthorised account for two years; (b) a false social-media rumour that the bank is insolvent causes customers to leave; (c) the bank is sued and fined for mis-selling insurance to clients; (d) the board launches a product in a country where it never becomes profitable. Which events are operational risk and which are not, and why?",
  "(a) Operational risk: internal fraud (failed people and controls), the Nick Leeson case. (c) Operational risk: legal risk is explicitly included, and the event type is clients, products and business practices. (b) Not operational risk: reputational risk is explicitly excluded because public perception cannot be reliably modelled, even when the damage is deliberate. (d) Not operational risk: strategic risk (a bad business decision, not a failure in execution) is excluded. So (a) and (c) are in; (b) and (d) are out.",
  [r"fraud|trader|\(?a\)?\s*(and|&|,)\s*\(?c\)?", r"legal|su(ed|it)|mis-?sell|\(?a\)?\s*(and|&|,)\s*\(?c\)?"]),
 ("A bank's loan book has an expected loss of R2bn a year, and in a 1-in-100 year losses could reach R9bn. The CFO proposes (i) raising impairment provisions to R9bn 'to be safe' and (ii) ignoring the extra R7bn when pricing loans 'because unexpected losses are outside the bank's control'. Correct both proposals.",
  "(i) Provisions and loan pricing (the credit risk premium) cover EXPECTED loss, the R2bn that is a normal, predictable cost of lending. The R7bn above that is unexpected loss, which is covered by capital, not provisions: absorbing it is what regulatory and economic capital are for. Provisioning R9bn would wipe out reported profit for a loss that occurs in only 1 year in 100. (ii) Unexpected loss is not uncontrollable and is not ignored: it is measured (PD models, VaR, duration-based capital at risk) and capital is held against it. That capital has a cost (the hurdle rate), so each loan must still earn enough on it; that is the RAROC test.",
  [r"capital", r"pric|premium|provision"]),
 ("Under the advanced measurement approach two banks of similar size and business mix reported operational risk capital of R2bn and R5bn. Explain why regulators saw this as a problem, name the approach that replaced the AMA under Basel III, and describe how it sets capital.",
  "The AMA let each bank use its own internal statistical models, so results depended heavily on modelling choices: similar banks produced very different capital for similar risks, the numbers could not be compared, banks had an incentive to choose models that minimised capital (gaming), and operational loss data is scarce and dominated by rare extreme events, so the models were unreliable in the tail. Basel III replaced all earlier approaches with the standardised measurement approach (SMA): capital is based on a business indicator (an income-based measure of the bank's size) scaled by the bank's own historical operational loss experience. It is simpler and comparable, while still rewarding a good loss record.",
  [r"\bsma\b|standardi[sz]ed measurement", r"business indicator|loss (history|experience|record)|compar|gam|model"]),
],

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Credit risk
"b-credit": [
 ("A manufacturer applying for a loan reports (R million): current assets 420, current liabilities 310, total assets 1 200, retained earnings 180, EBIT 96, total liabilities 800, sales 1 500. Its market capitalisation is 540. Using Z = 1.2X1 + 1.4X2 + 3.3X3 + 0.6X4 + 1.0X5, calculate the Altman Z-score (2 decimals).",
  "2.24. X1 = (420 - 310) / 1 200 = 0.0917; X2 = 180 / 1 200 = 0.15; X3 = 96 / 1 200 = 0.08; X4 = 540 / 800 = 0.675 (market value of equity over book value of total liabilities); X5 = 1 500 / 1 200 = 1.25. Z = 0.110 + 0.210 + 0.264 + 0.405 + 1.250 = 2.24. Grey zone (1.81 to 2.99): neither lend nor decline on the score alone; look further at qualitative factors (management, collateral, the business cycle). Traps: using current assets instead of working capital for X1, or book equity (400) for X4.", 2.24),
 ("Zero-coupon Treasury yields are 7.5% for one year and 8.2% for two years. Zero-coupon yields on B-rated corporate debt are 10.4% for one year and 11.9% for two years. Using the term structure approach, what is the cumulative probability that the corporate defaults within two years (percent, 2 decimals)?",
  "6.50%. Year 1: p1 = 1.075 / 1.104 = 0.9737 (PD 2.63%). Forward rates for year 2: Treasury 1.082^2 / 1.075 = 1.0890; corporate 1.119^2 / 1.104 = 1.1342. p2 = 1.0890 / 1.1342 = 0.9602 (marginal PD 3.98%). Cumulative PD = 1 - 0.9737 x 0.9602 = 6.50%. Trap: adding 2.63% and 3.98% gives 6.61%; the year-2 PD applies only to survivors. Assumptions: the whole spread is default risk, zero recovery, risk-neutral investors.", 6.5),
 ("A bank prices a loan at a base rate of 11.75% plus a risk premium of 3.25%, with a 0.5% origination fee and a 12% compensating balance; the reserve requirement is 2.5%. The borrower refuses the compensating balance. With the same fee and base rate, what risk premium would give the bank the same contractually promised return per rand lent (percent, 2 decimals)?",
  "5.30%. With the balance: 1 + k = 1 + (0.005 + 0.1175 + 0.0325) / [1 - 0.12 x (1 - 0.025)] = 1 + 0.155 / 0.883, so k = 17.55%. Without it the denominator is 1, so k = fee + base rate + premium: premium = 17.55% - 0.5% - 11.75% = 5.30%. The compensating balance was worth about 2.05 points of premium. Note that reserves matter only through the compensating balance, and that k is a promised return that ignores default risk; that is what RAROC adds.", 5.3),
 ("A bank has made a R80m fixed-rate loan with a duration of 4.4 years at a yield of 10%. To hedge a possible downgrade it sells a credit forward on R80m at a forward credit spread of 2.4%. At maturity the borrower has been upgraded and the spread is 1.6%. What is the bank's cash flow on the forward (rand; negative if the bank pays)?",
  "-R2 560 000. D* = 4.4 / 1.10 = 4.0. The spread narrowed, so the bank (the seller) pays the buyer: (2.4% - 1.6%) x 4.0 x 80 000 000 = R2 560 000. The loan has gained about the same amount in value, so the bank is no worse off overall, but it gave away the upside. Its maximum loss on the forward is reached if the spread falls to zero: 2.4% x 4.0 x 80m = R7.68m. A digital default option would instead have cost only a premium, but it pays out only on actual default, not on a downgrade.", -2560000),
 ("A bank's logit model is Y = -4.1 + 1.3(D/E) - 0.9(S/A), with PD = 1 / (1 + exp(-Y)). An applicant has debt/equity of 2.5 and sales/assets of 0.9 and wants a R20m loan against collateral expected to recover 55%. What is the expected loss on the loan (R million, 2 decimals)?",
  "R1.44m. Y = -4.1 + 3.25 - 0.81 = -1.66. PD = 1 / (1 + e^1.66) = 1 / 6.259 = 15.98%. LGD = 1 - 0.55 = 45%. EL = 15.98% x 45% x 20 = R1.44m, i.e. 7.2% of the loan, which the risk premium would have to cover. The coefficient 1.3 is the change in the log-odds of default for a one-unit rise in D/E, not the change in PD. Unlike a linear probability model, the logit keeps PD between 0 and 1.", 1.44),
 ("A one-year transition matrix shows that a BBB borrower ends the year as: A 4%, BBB 86%, BB 9%, default 1%. One-year default rates are 0.2% for A and 9% for BB. Assuming the matrix is the same each year, what is the probability that a borrower rated BBB today defaults within two years (percent, 2 decimals)?",
  "2.68%. Year 1 default: 1.00%. Year 2 default depends on where the borrower ended year 1: stayed BBB 86% x 1% = 0.86%; downgraded to BB 9% x 9% = 0.81%; upgraded to A 4% x 0.2% = 0.008%. Total = 1.00 + 0.86 + 0.81 + 0.008 = 2.68%. Trap: 1 - 0.99^2 = 1.99% ignores migration; almost a third of the two-year risk comes from the downgrade path, which is why migration analysis gives early warning before defaults show up.", 2.68),
 ("A construction company with a debt/equity ratio that has risen to 3, volatile earnings and a past rescheduling of debt asks for a R200m loan secured on specialised equipment with a book value of R200m. A recession is forecast. The loan officer says the bank's risk is fully covered by the collateral. Is he right? Give two reasons, and identify the borrower-specific and market-specific factors that push the risk premium up.",
  "No. Collateral is worth only its market value, not its book value, and specialised equipment is illiquid: in a default it would be sold quickly at fire-sale prices. Collateral values also fall exactly when defaults happen (in a downturn), and recording errors can weaken the claim. Collateral lowers LGD; it does not reduce the probability of default. Premium-raising factors: borrower-specific are high leverage (larger prior claims on cash flow), volatile earnings (cash flow may fall below debt service in bad periods) and poor reputation (the rescheduling); market-specific is the business cycle (a recession, and construction is cyclical), plus the level of interest rates if they are high.",
  [NO, r"market value|fire.?sale|illiquid|fall|downturn|recession|book value"]),
 ("A bank made a five-year fixed-rate loan to an A-rated firm last year. It now fears the firm will be downgraded to BB, though not that it will default. Which credit derivative suits this fear, which side does the bank take, how is the payoff calculated, and why would a digital default option not do the job?",
  "A credit forward, which hedges an increase in default risk after the loan rate has been set. The bank SELLS the forward at today's forward credit spread; if the spread at maturity is wider, the buyer pays the bank (spread at maturity - forward spread) x modified duration x notional, offsetting the fall in the loan's value. If the spread narrows, the bank pays, so it gives up the upside. A digital default option pays a fixed amount (par) only if the borrower actually defaults; a downgrade without default leaves the bank with a less valuable loan and no payout. (A CDS also responds only to a credit event.)",
  [r"credit forward|forward", r"sell|short"]),
],

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Interest rate risk
"b-irr": [
 ("A bank's balance sheet (R million). Assets: cash 20; 3-month Treasury bills 60; overdrafts 90; 25-year floating-rate mortgages 240; 9-month fixed-rate commercial loans 70; 3-year fixed-rate loans 150; 10-year fixed-rate government bonds 80. Liabilities and equity: floating-rate call deposits 250; 6-month certificates of deposit 160; 1-year time deposits 90; 2-year fixed deposits 70; 5-year bonds issued 70; equity 70. Using a one-year horizon, what is the change in annual NII if all rates rise by 1.5 percentage points (R million, 1 decimal; negative for a fall)?",
  "-R0.6m. ISA = 60 + 90 + 240 + 70 = 460 (the mortgages are floating, so they reprice whatever their maturity; the 9-month loans roll over within the year). ISL = 250 + 160 + 90 = 500. CGAP = 460 - 500 = -40. Change in NII = -40 x 1.5% = -R0.6m. Excluded: cash, the 3-year loans, the 10-year bonds, the 2-year deposits, the 5-year bonds and equity. Trap: classifying by maturity instead of by repricing date puts the mortgages in the wrong place and reverses the sign. A negative gap means refinancing risk: the bank is hurt by rising rates.", -0.6),
 ("A bank reports these repricing buckets (R million): 0 to 3 months: ISA 120, ISL 200; 3 to 6 months: ISA 90, ISL 70; 6 to 12 months: ISA 60, ISL 100. The yield curve flattens: rates rise by 2.0 percentage points for the first bucket, 1.5 points for the second and 1.0 point for the third. What is the annualised change in NII (R million, 1 decimal; negative for a fall)?",
  "-R1.7m. Gaps: -80, +20 and -40 (cumulative one-year gap -100). Change in NII = (-80 x 2.0%) + (20 x 1.5%) + (-40 x 1.0%) = -1.6 + 0.3 - 0.4 = -R1.7m. Trap: applying one rate change to the cumulative gap (-100 x 2% = -2.0, or -100 x 1.5% = -1.5) is only right for a parallel shift; when rates move by different amounts, use each bucket's own gap and rate change.", -1.7),
 ("A bank has a one-year repricing gap of -R600m. It enters a swap on a notional of R400m in which it pays a fixed 8.0% and receives a floating rate, currently also 8.0%. All rates then rise by 0.75 percentage points. What is the net change in the bank's annual income, including the swap (R million, 1 decimal; negative for a fall)?",
  "-R1.5m. Unhedged: -600 x 0.75% = -R4.5m. Swap: receives floating 8.75% x 400 = 35.0 and pays fixed 8.0% x 400 = 32.0, a net +R3.0m (it was zero before the rise). Net = -4.5 + 3.0 = -R1.5m, which is the unhedged part of the gap: -200 x 0.75%. A negative gap is hedged by paying fixed and receiving floating; to hedge fully the notional must equal the gap of R600m.", -1.5),
 ("A bank with a repricing gap of -R500m must choose between (a) a swap on R500m paying fixed 7.5% and receiving floating, and (b) a cap on R500m with a 7.5% strike costing a premium of 0.3% of the notional. The floating rate is 7.5% today. Suppose rates then FALL by 1 percentage point. Over the year, by how much is the bank better off with the cap than with the swap (R million, 1 decimal)?",
  "R3.5m. The negative gap gains when rates fall: -500 x -1% = +R5.0m. Swap: receives 6.5% and pays 7.5% on 500, a loss of R5.0m, so the net result is zero: the swap locks the bank in both ways. Cap: no payoff (the rate is below the strike), cost = premium 0.3% x 500 = R1.5m, so the net result is +R3.5m. Difference = R3.5m. The cap is one-sided protection that keeps the benefit of falling rates, at the cost of an upfront premium; had rates risen, the swap would have been better by the R1.5m premium.", 3.5),
 ("A bank holds R50m face value of a three-year bond paying a 10% annual coupon, priced to yield 12%. It expects yields to rise by 1.5 percentage points. Using Macaulay duration and change in P = -D x P x change in y / (1 + y), what is the expected fall in the value of the position (R million, 2 decimals)?",
  "R1.74m. Per R100 face: PVs are 10 / 1.12 = 8.929, 10 / 1.12^2 = 7.972 and 110 / 1.12^3 = 78.296; price = 95.196. Duration = (8.929 x 1 + 7.972 x 2 + 78.296 x 3) / 95.196 = 2.729 years. Position value = R47.598m. Fall = 2.729 x 47.598 x 0.015 / 1.12 = R1.74m. Hedge: sell a forward (or futures) on R50m face of the bond at today's price. Traps: applying duration to the R50m face value instead of the market value, and forgetting to divide by (1 + y).", 1.74),
 ("A bank's assets have a market value of R900m and a duration of 4.0 years; its liabilities have a market value of R820m and a duration of 1.4 years. Yields on both are 10%. Using change in value = -D x value x change in y / (1 + y) for each side, what is the change in the market value of the bank's equity if rates rise by 1 percentage point (R million, 2 decimals; negative for a fall)?",
  "-R22.29m. Assets: -4.0 x 900 x 0.01 / 1.10 = -32.73. Liabilities: -1.4 x 820 x 0.01 / 1.10 = -10.44 (a fall in the value of liabilities is a gain to the bank). Change in equity = -32.73 - (-10.44) = -R22.29m, 28% of the R80m of equity from a single 1-point move. This is market value risk: it hits capital (net worth), not NII, and arises because assets are longer than liabilities. Trap: applying the difference in durations (4.0 - 1.4) to one balance ignores the different sizes of the two sides.", -22.29),
 ("A bank funds a three-year fixed-rate loan at 12% with one-year deposits costing 9%. After a year, deposit rates have risen to 12.5%. (a) What is the spread in year 1 and in year 2? (b) Which form of interest rate risk is this, and which sign of repricing gap goes with it? (c) Give one on-balance-sheet and one derivative hedge.",
  "(a) Year 1: 12% - 9% = +3%. Year 2: 12% - 12.5% = -0.5%; the loan rate is locked in, the funding cost is not. (b) Refinancing risk: long-term assets funded by short-term liabilities that must be rolled over, which is a negative repricing gap (ISA < ISL); the bank is hurt by rising rates. (c) On balance sheet: fund the loan with a three-year fixed deposit or bond (match maturities), or make the loan floating-rate. Derivative: a pay-fixed, receive-floating swap with notional equal to the gap, or buy a cap, or sell interest rate futures.",
  [r"refinanc", r"negative|isa\s*<\s*isl|ris(e|es|ing)|increas"]),
 ("A bank has a one-year cumulative gap of +R2bn and expects the SARB to cut rates by 1 percentage point. The treasurer proposes a pay-fixed, receive-floating swap on R2bn 'to protect NII'. Evaluate the proposal: what is the unhedged effect of the cut, what does the proposed swap do, and what should the bank do instead?",
  "Unhedged: change in NII = +2bn x -1% = -R20m. A positive gap loses when rates fall (reinvestment risk). The proposed swap is the hedge for a NEGATIVE gap: when rates fall the bank receives less floating while still paying fixed, losing roughly another R20m, so it doubles the exposure instead of removing it. The right hedge is to receive fixed and pay floating on R2bn, or to buy a floor; on balance sheet, make more fixed-rate loans or take more floating-rate funding. The endowment effect points the same way: SA banks tend to earn less when rates fall.",
  [r"receiv\w*\s+(the\s+|a\s+)?fixed|floor", r"wrong|not|incorrect|worse|double|opposite|negative"]),
],

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Market risk
"b-mkt": [
 ("A trading book holds R60m of equities with a daily volatility of 1.4% and a R40m currency position with a daily volatility of 0.9%. The correlation between them is 0.3. Using the parametric method with z = 2.33, what is the 10-day 99% VaR of the portfolio (R million, 2 decimals)?",
  "R7.43m. One-day VaRs: equities 2.33 x 1.4% x 60 = 1.957; currency 2.33 x 0.9% x 40 = 0.839. Portfolio = square root of (1.957^2 + 0.839^2 + 2 x 0.3 x 1.957 x 0.839) = square root of 5.519 = R2.349m. Ten-day VaR = 2.349 x square root of 10 = R7.43m. Trap: adding the two VaRs gives R2.796m a day (R8.84m over 10 days), which is only right if the correlation is 1; the difference is the diversification benefit.", 7.43),
 ("Share A returns -6%, 4% or 12% and share B returns 8%, 3% or -2% in a recession, normal growth or a boom, with probabilities 0.3, 0.4 and 0.3. What is the standard deviation of a portfolio invested 50% in each (percent, 2 decimals)?",
  "1.57%. Portfolio return in each state: recession 0.5 x -6 + 0.5 x 8 = 1.0%; normal 3.5%; boom 5.0%. Expected return = 0.3 x 1.0 + 0.4 x 3.5 + 0.3 x 5.0 = 3.2%. Variance = 0.3 x (-2.2)^2 + 0.4 x 0.3^2 + 0.3 x 1.8^2 = 1.452 + 0.036 + 0.972 = 2.46; standard deviation = 1.57%. The shares alone have standard deviations of 6.99% and 3.87%; because they move in opposite directions (correlation close to -1), the portfolio is far less risky than either. Trap: averaging the two standard deviations gives 5.43%.", 1.57),
 ("Share X returns -5%, 6% or 15% and share Y returns 1%, 4% or 7%, with probabilities 0.25, 0.50 and 0.25. A risk-averse investor chooses the share with the lower risk per unit of expected return. What is the coefficient of variation of the share she chooses (2 decimals)?",
  "0.53 (share Y). X: expected return = -1.25 + 3.0 + 3.75 = 5.5%; variance = 0.25 x 10.5^2 + 0.5 x 0.5^2 + 0.25 x 9.5^2 = 50.25; standard deviation 7.09%; CV = 7.09 / 5.5 = 1.29. Y: expected return 4.0%; variance = 0.25 x 9 + 0 + 0.25 x 9 = 4.5; standard deviation 2.12%; CV = 2.12 / 4.0 = 0.53. Y has the lower CV, so it is chosen even though X has the higher expected return. A risk seeker would pick X for its higher standard deviation.", 0.53),
 ("A bank's average 1-day 99% VaR over the last 60 trading days is R4m, and yesterday's 1-day 99% VaR was R15m after a market shock. Back-testing over 250 days shows 7 exceptions. The multiplier k is 3.00 for 0 to 4 exceptions, 3.40 for 5, 3.50 for 6, 3.65 for 7, 3.75 for 8, 3.85 for 9 and 4.00 for 10 or more. What is the market risk charge (R million, 2 decimals)?",
  "R47.43m. The charge uses 10-day VaR: average = 4 x square root of 10 = 12.649; yesterday = 15 x square root of 10 = 47.434. With 7 exceptions (yellow zone) k = 3.65, so k x average = 3.65 x 12.649 = 46.169. MRC = the greater of 46.169 and 47.434 = R47.43m: yesterday's VaR binds. Traps: forgetting to scale to 10 days, applying k to yesterday's VaR, or stopping at k x average without checking the second leg.", 47.43),
 ("A bank's 1-day 99% VaR is R5.2m. Its book consists of 2 million shares quoted at R49.60 bid and R50.40 offer, and bonds with a mid-market value of R200m quoted at 99.80 bid and 100.20 offer per R100. What is the liquidity-adjusted VaR in a normal market (R million, 2 decimals)?",
  "R6.40m. Shares: mid R50, position R100m, proportional spread s = 0.80 / 50 = 1.6%, cost = 0.5 x 1.6% x 100 = R0.80m. Bonds: s = 0.40 / 100 = 0.4%, cost = 0.5 x 0.4% x 200 = R0.40m. Liquidation cost = R1.20m. LVaR = 5.2 + 1.2 = R6.40m. A sale takes place half a spread below mid, so the cost is half the spread, not the full spread (which would give R7.60m). In a stressed market the spread is replaced by its mean plus 2.33 standard deviations.", 6.4),
 ("A bank holds a bond position worth R150m with a modified duration of 6.0. The daily volatility of yield changes is 0.08 percentage points (8 basis points). Using z = 1.65, what is the 5-day 95% VaR (R million, 2 decimals)?",
  "R2.66m. Daily VaR = position value x modified duration x adverse yield move = 150 x 6.0 x (1.65 x 0.0008) = R1.188m. Five-day VaR = 1.188 x square root of 5 = R2.66m. These are the three components of daily earnings at risk: rand value, price sensitivity and the potential adverse move in yield. Traps: using z = 2.33 (that is 99%), multiplying by 5 instead of the square root of 5, or treating 0.08 points as 8%.", 2.66),
 ("Two desks each report a 1-day 99% VaR of R20m. Desk A holds shares and has an expected shortfall of R23m. Desk B has sold options and has an expected shortfall of R55m. The head of trading says the two desks carry the same risk and proposes measuring both with parametric VaR. What is wrong with both statements?",
  "VaR is only a threshold: both desks lose more than R20m on 1 day in 100, but VaR says nothing about how bad that day is. Expected shortfall is the average loss beyond the VaR: R23m for A but R55m for B, so B has far more tail risk and needs more capital. That is why VaR failed in 2008/9 and why Basel now uses ES. Parametric VaR assumes normal returns and suits only linear positions; sold options have non-linear payoffs and fat tails, so desk B needs historical simulation or Monte Carlo (full revaluation), backed by stress testing.",
  [r"tail|beyond|expected shortfall|\bes\b|average", r"historical|monte carlo|non.?linear|option|normal"]),
 ("A bank back-tests its 1-day 99% VaR model over the last 250 trading days and finds 12 days on which the loss exceeded the VaR. How many exceptions should it have expected, what does the result say about the model, and what are the regulatory consequences?",
  "A 99% VaR should be exceeded on about 1% of days: 2.5 days in 250. Twelve exceptions is nearly five times that, so the model understates risk (for example it assumes normal returns and misses fat tails, or uses a look-back window that was too calm). Ten or more exceptions put the bank in the red zone: the multiplier k rises from 3 to its maximum of 4, so the market risk charge rises by a third, and the supervisor may disallow the model altogether. The bank should also stress test, since VaR is built on normal conditions.",
  [r"red|\b4(\.0+)?\b|disallow|multiplier", r"understat|underestimat|inaccurate|too low|not accurate"]),
],

}
